user can form a chow of T5-T6-T7, but they don't have T7. So not possible. \n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe the best move is to discard T8, as it's not contributing to any melds and could be a liability. Even though the hand isn't a winning one yet, discarding T8 might help in future draws or prevent others from using it. Alternatively, discarding J2 might be bad because it's a pair. So the best option is to play T8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has a chow of B2-B3-B4 and a pair of J2. To form a winning hand, three more melds are needed. The W7, W8, W9 can form a chow, but T5, T6, T8 lack a sequence. Discarding T8 removes a non-essential tile, preserving potential for forming additional melds.]\nReason: [Discarding T8 eliminates a tile that does not contribute to any potential melds, allowing the hand to retain key tiles for forming chows (W7-W8-W9) and pairs (J2, W8/W9). This improves the chances of completing the required four melds and a pair.]\nAnswer: [Play T8]"}
